<% content_for(:title, "Navbar Review") %>

<style>
  /* Preview container base */
  .navbar-preview {
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The frame is the simulated phone's BEZEL, not part of its screen. The
       slider and the label both count the border box, while a percentage inside
       the preview resolves against the CONTENT box — so every width expression
       below adds the frame back. Named rather than spelled 4px twice. */
    --preview-frame: 2px;
    border: var(--preview-frame) solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* --nav-p is a registered <number>, so the Scrolled toggle can TRANSITION
       it directly — one interpolating property in place of the five per-element
       font-size/width/padding transitions this block used to carry. */
    transition: width 0.15s ease, --nav-p 0.3s ease;
  }
  .navbar-preview header { position: static !important; }

  /* Mobile previews: force mobile layout (undo md: classes active at desktop viewport) */
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .hidden.md\:flex { display: none !important; }
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .hidden.md\:block { display: none !important; }
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .flex.md\:hidden { display: flex !important; }
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .user-nav-col { padding-left: 0 !important; padding-right: 1rem !important; }
  <%# align-items rides along with flex-direction, and leaving it out made this
      preview draw a header no phone draws. A column container aligns on the
      horizontal axis, so the shipped mobile band resets the base baseline to
      stretch to give the wordmark's spans a box narrower than their own text to
      truncate against. Without it here the preview shows the words at full
      max-content width, spilling over the user column, which is the defect the
      shipped rule fixes. %>
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .nav-title { flex-direction: column !important; gap: 0 !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; align-items: stretch !important; }
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .nav-title span:first-child { margin-bottom: -4px !important; }
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .nav-logo-link { gap: 0.5rem !important; }

  /* Breakpoint simulation for the --nav-p sizes. The live navbar picks these
     up from media queries in application.css, which cannot fire for a
     CONTAINER at a fixed viewport width — so the same calc()s are re-keyed on
     the wrapper. They are the same expressions, not a second set of numbers:
     if you retune the collapse, retune both or the preview starts lying.
     Unlayered, so they beat @layer utilities' .nav-shell without !important. */
  .navbar-preview.is-mobile .nav-shell {
    --nav-logo-size: calc(3rem - 0.5rem * var(--nav-p));
  }
  .navbar-preview.bp-tiny .nav-shell {
    --nav-title-size: calc(1.1rem - 0.2rem * var(--nav-p));
    --nav-title-lead-size: calc(1.3rem - 0.3rem * var(--nav-p));
  }
  .navbar-preview.bp-small .nav-shell {
    --nav-title-size: calc(1.25rem - 0.25rem * var(--nav-p));
    --nav-title-lead-size: calc(1.5rem - 0.35rem * var(--nav-p));
  }

  /* THE USER-COLUMN WIDTH STEPS, re-keyed on the wrapper for the same reason the
     --nav-p sizes above are: the shipped rules live in media queries, and a media
     query cannot fire for a CONTAINER at a fixed viewport width.

     PERCENT HERE, vw THERE, AND THEY ARE THE SAME NUMBER. application.css caps
     the two phone bands at clamp(floor, 100vw minus what the left column needs,
     ceiling). A literal 100vw inside a 390px preview box on a 1400px monitor
     resolves to 1400px, which would draw a header no phone has — so the
     measurement is taken against the CONTAINING BLOCK, which in this preview is
     the navbar row, i.e. the simulated device width. Both numbers inside the
     clamp are read straight out of the shipped rule
     (var(--user-nav-floor), var(--nav-left-need)), so the floor is declared
     exactly once in this app and this block cannot drift from it.

     BOTH PHONE BANDS RESTATE --nav-left-need, and the tiny one is the reason
     this sentence exists. A reviewer opens this page on a DESKTOP, where the
     shipped `@media (min-width: 400px)` query fires for real and sets the need
     to its 400px-band value on every preview on the page — so a tiny preview
     that merely inherited the property drew its column with the WRONG band's
     number. Measured: 190px against the 212px a real 390px phone gets, an 18px
     lie, with every other expression here already correct. Each band restates
     its own need for the same reason it restates its own ceiling.

     The `+ 2 * var(--preview-frame)` adds the preview's own border back. The
     slider and the label count the border box; a percentage resolves against
     the content box, so without it every column here is 4px narrow — and the
     desktop band's `0.48 * var(--preview-frame)` is 24% of the same 4px.

     THE DESKTOP STEP NOW SAYS WHAT SHIPS TOO. It read a flat 20rem while the
     shipped band is clamp(16rem, 24vw, 20rem) — so the Tablet preview at 1032px
     drew a 320px column where production draws 256px, a 64px lie in the
     reviewer's own instrument.

     REMOVED, 2026-09-28: three `.username-cap` rules, at 5/6/7rem. No element in
     this app has ever carried that class — components/_user_nav caps the name
     with `max-w-[4.5rem] min-[400px]:max-w-[6rem] md:max-w-[7rem]` and fades the
     clipped edge through navUsernameFade's mask. The rules were dead the day they
     were written, they disagreed with the live 4.5rem at the tiny band, and they
     read to the next person sizing this header like a cap already in force.
     studio-engine dropped its own three the same way in 0.77.1. */

  /* Tiny (< 400px) */
  .navbar-preview.bp-tiny .nav-logo-link { gap: 0.25rem !important; }
  .navbar-preview.bp-tiny .user-nav-col { --nav-left-need: 11.125rem; width: clamp(var(--user-nav-floor), calc(100% + 2 * var(--preview-frame) - var(--nav-left-need)), 14rem) !important; }

  /* Small (400–767px) */
  .navbar-preview.bp-small .user-nav-col { --nav-left-need: 12.25rem; width: clamp(var(--user-nav-floor), calc(100% + 2 * var(--preview-frame) - var(--nav-left-need)), 15rem) !important; }

  /* Desktop (768px+) */
  .navbar-preview.is-desktop .user-nav-col { width: clamp(16rem, calc(24% + 0.48 * var(--preview-frame)), 20rem) !important; }

  /* Scrolled state. The size half of this used to be TWELVE !important rules
     restating every collapsed value; the toggle now just sets --nav-p: 1 on
     the wrapper (below) and the shipped calc()s do the rest, so the preview
     exercises the real rules instead of a parallel copy of them. What is left
     is the shadow, which the live header gets from .is-scrolled and a preview
     header (no x-data, so no `scrolled`) cannot. */
  .navbar-preview.is-scrolled-preview header { box-shadow: 0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .navbar-preview header { transition: box-shadow 0.3s, border-color 0.3s; }

  /* Slider with device marker */
  .slider-wrap { position: relative; }
  .slider-wrap input[type="range"] { width: 100%; accent-color: var(--color-cta); }
  .navbar-card { margin-bottom: 1px; }
  .slider-marker {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: 2px;
    height: 14px;
    background: var(--color-cta);
    pointer-events: none;
    border-radius: 1px;
  }
</style>

<div class="py-8 space-y-10" x-data="{
       previewName: '',
       updateNames() {
         var els = document.querySelectorAll('.navbar-preview [data-username-display]');
         var name = this.previewName;
         els.forEach(function(el) {
           el.textContent = name || el.getAttribute('data-original-name');
           var data = Alpine.$data(el);
           if (data) { data.overflows = el.scrollWidth > el.clientWidth; }
         });
       },
       init() {
         var els = document.querySelectorAll('.navbar-preview [data-username-display]');
         els.forEach(function(el) { el.setAttribute('data-original-name', el.textContent.trim()); });
       }
     }" x-effect="updateNames()">
  <div>
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading">Navbar Review</h1>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-1">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">Desktop (1200px+) is the live navbar above.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <label class="text-xs text-muted font-medium">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" x-model="previewName" placeholder="<%= current_user.display_name %>"
               class="input-field text-xs !py-1 !px-2 !w-36">
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <% breakpoints = [
    { name: "Tiny",   min: 320, max: 399, classes: "is-mobile bp-tiny",  device: "iPhone 15", device_w: 390 },
    { name: "Small",  min: 400, max: 767, classes: "is-mobile bp-small", device: "iPhone 16 Pro Max", device_w: 430 },
    { name: "Tablet", min: 768, max: 1200, classes: "is-desktop",        device: "iPad Pro 13\"", device_w: 1032 },
  ] %>

  <% [
    { title: "Logged-In View",  show_logged_in: true },
    { title: "Pre-Login View",  show_logged_in: false },
  ].each do |section| %>
    <section class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-heading"><%= section[:title] %></h2>
      <div class="grid gap-8">
        <% breakpoints.each do |bp|
             marker_pct = ((bp[:device_w] - bp[:min]).to_f / (bp[:max] - bp[:min]) * 100).round(1)
        %>
          <div class="navbar-card" data-controller="navbar-preview"
               data-navbar-preview-device-width-value="<%= bp[:device_w] %>"
               data-action="turbo:before-cache@document->navbar-preview#reset">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
              <span class="text-sm font-bold text-heading"><%= bp[:name] %></span>
              <span class="text-xs text-muted font-mono" data-navbar-preview-target="label"><%= bp[:device_w] %>px</span>
              <div class="flex-1 max-w-xs slider-wrap">
                <input type="range" min="<%= bp[:min] %>" max="<%= bp[:max] %>" value="<%= bp[:device_w] %>"
                       data-navbar-preview-target="slider" data-action="input->navbar-preview#resize">
                <div class="slider-marker" style="left: <%= marker_pct %>%;"></div>
              </div>
              <button type="button" data-action="navbar-preview#fit" class="text-xs text-primary hover:text-primary-300 font-medium transition whitespace-nowrap">
                <%= bp[:device] %> (<%= bp[:device_w] %>px)
              </button>
              <span class="text-xs text-muted font-mono"><%= bp[:min] %>–<%= bp[:max] %>px</span>
              <button type="button" data-navbar-preview-target="toggle" data-action="navbar-preview#toggleScrolled"
                      class="text-xs font-bold px-2 py-0.5 rounded transition bg-surface-alt text-secondary hover:text-heading">
                Scrolled
              </button>
            </div>
            <div class="navbar-preview <%= bp[:classes] %>" data-navbar-preview-target="frame" style="width: <%= bp[:device_w] %>px; --nav-p: 0">
              <%= render "layouts/navbar", preview: true, show_logged_in: section[:show_logged_in] %>
            </div>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </section>
  <% end %>
</div>
